a(f"`conflict_priority.csv` maps all **107 conflicted colour records** to their conflict group and scores **{len(G)} colour-level groups** ({st['tier_counts'].get('A', 0)} tier A, {st['tier_counts'].get('B', 0)} tier B, {st['tier_counts'].get('C', 0)} tier C); the other {st['cross_cutting']} of the 39 carried conflict rows are cross-cutting (lineage, certificate colour class, label location, finish type, name-match review, matte pairs, named conflicts) and are listed unscored. Records by tier: A {rec_t.get('A', 0)}, B {rec_t.get('B', 0)}, C {rec_t.get('C', 0)}.")
a("")
a("**Score** = 2 × popularity proxy + competition + matte/gloss + route plausibility + genuine disagreement + size. *Popularity proxy* (3 vs 1) is membership in your priority series (Seal, Dolphin, Seagull, Atto 3/Yuan PLUS, Han, Qin PLUS, Song PLUS, Destroyer 05, e2) because the repository holds no sales data. *Competition* counts distinct **vehicle** codes (formula-style identifiers excluded). *Matte/gloss* = involvement of a code in an OEMatch/DipYourCar gloss-vs-matte pair (one lineage - a hypothesis, not evidence). *Route plausibility* = 3 on-sale BYD-brand model reachable through the Dynasty/Ocean network, 2 older BYD model, 1 separate brand network. *Genuine disagreement* = 2 if lineages' code sets are not nested, 1 if nested, 0 for alias groups. *Size* = colour records affected (max 3). **No conflict is resolved, by majority vote or otherwise, and sites sharing one upstream are never counted as extra evidence.**")
